WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.623
Module Ten, Lesson One: three oversight models, and where each one belongs.

2
00:00:04.953 --> 00:00:07.882
Where you put the human is an architecture decision,

3
00:00:07.932 --> 00:00:10.002
with real costs in latency and staff.

4
00:00:10.802 --> 00:00:13.936
Picture a cloud operations agent at an online store,

5
00:00:13.986 --> 00:00:16.875
running thousands of infrastructure tasks a day.

6
00:00:17.205 --> 00:00:20.031
It tags resources, scales servers up and down,

7
00:00:20.081 --> 00:00:22.595
and now and then deletes an old database.

8
00:00:23.395 --> 00:00:26.267
Should a person approve every single one of those actions?

9
00:00:26.597 --> 00:00:30.238
The agent would stall, and the reviewers would soon approve without looking.

10
00:00:31.038 --> 00:00:34.182
Instead, ask two questions: can this action be undone,

11
00:00:34.232 --> 00:00:36.193
and how much damage if it's wrong?

12
00:00:36.523 --> 00:00:39.055
The answers point to one of three oversight models.

13
00:00:39.855 --> 00:00:42.130
Human-in-the-loop means the agent pauses,

14
00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.626
and can't proceed without explicit approval.

15
00:00:44.956 --> 00:00:48.578
It costs the most, so save it for irreversible, high-value,

16
00:00:48.628 --> 00:00:53.183
regulated or safety-critical actions, like deleting a production database.

17
00:00:53.983 --> 00:00:56.143
Human-on-the-loop lets the agent act,

18
00:00:56.193 --> 00:00:59.309
while a person monitors and can intervene or reverse.

19
00:00:59.639 --> 00:01:03.856
Scaling servers fits here: it happens at volume, and there's a working undo.

20
00:01:04.656 --> 00:01:07.241
Human-in-command means people set the policy,

21
00:01:07.291 --> 00:01:10.110
scope and limits, with no per-action involvement.

22
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.690
It's the cheapest model, for low-stakes,

23
00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.681
reversible, high-volume work like tagging resources.

24
00:01:16.481 --> 00:01:20.039
Technically, an approval is an interrupt at the tool boundary:

25
00:01:20.089 --> 00:01:24.345
the agent pauses before executing and saves its state with a checkpointer.

26
00:01:24.675 --> 00:01:28.999
LangGraph does this directly, with interrupt before and a durable checkpointer.

27
00:01:29.799 --> 00:01:33.750
The reviewer sees the proposed action with its reasoning and evidence,

28
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:35.807
and can approve, reject, or edit it.

29
00:01:36.137 --> 00:01:39.181
On approval, the agent resumes from that exact point.

30
00:01:39.981 --> 00:01:44.837
Set a timeout policy for when nobody responds, and never let it silently proceed.

31
00:01:45.167 --> 00:01:48.851
Batch similar approvals, so reviewers aren't interrupted constantly.

32
00:01:49.651 --> 00:01:53.964
The agent also needs escalation triggers that name concrete conditions,

33
00:01:54.014 --> 00:01:55.254
not just when unsure.

34
00:01:55.584 --> 00:01:58.214
Think confidence below a calibrated threshold,

35
00:01:58.264 --> 00:02:01.827
the same step failing twice, or a request that's out of scope.

36
00:02:02.627 --> 00:02:04.710
Others are a value or risk limit,

37
00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:09.297
a frustrated user asking for a person, or a rule that requires a human.

38
00:02:09.627 --> 00:02:11.839
Each one is a clear signal to hand over.

39
00:02:12.639 --> 00:02:16.343
When it escalates, hand off context, not just the conversation.

40
00:02:16.673 --> 00:02:18.842
The human gets the goal, what was tried,

41
00:02:18.892 --> 00:02:21.893
what was found, the sources, and why the agent stopped.

42
00:02:22.693 --> 00:02:26.087
On the exam, choose by reversibility and blast radius:

43
00:02:26.137 --> 00:02:29.085
irreversible and high impact means in the loop.

44
00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:31.887
Approving everything is a real wrong answer,

45
00:02:31.937 --> 00:02:35.040
because it doesn't scale and it breeds rubber-stamping.

46
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.279
And a handoff that makes the customer repeat

47
00:02:38.329 --> 00:02:40.654
everything is the failure users hate most.

48
00:02:40.984 --> 00:02:44.817
Read on for the full comparison table and every escalation trigger.
